Fixes to the Kotlin reader of lizard 1.26.1.

- `type` is a plain name. Read as Go's keyword, `add(type)` skipped the `)`
  and the next token, often a function's closing `}`.
- A raw string is one token that ends at the last quote of its closing run,
  and a backtick name may hold spaces, as test names do.
- An expression body ends with its line unless the line goes on, by an
  operator at its end or at the start of the next line, or by an open
  bracket. A block inside it, a lambda or an object body, is read like a
  function body, so a `fun` inside is a function of its own.
- A declaration without a body, an abstract or interface member, is not a
  function, and no longer takes the body of the next one.
- A lambda or function type arrow starts no anonymous function; only a
  `when` branch arrow counts as a branch.
- A declaration header may span lines; an extension function is named
  `Type.name`.
- A comma inside a parameter's type or default value, `(Int, Int) -> Int` or
  `Map<K, V>`, does not split the parameter.
- `get` and `set` start an accessor only when `(` follows them.
- The members of an `interface` are read as a class's are: a member with a
  body is a function of its own.
- An expression body ends on the line of its last token, and the token after
  it counts toward the enclosing code.

